Make filenames sane: unix-safe, portable, readable. Preview by default.

Usage: detoxrs [OPTIONS] <PATH>...
       detoxrs <COMMAND>

Commands:
  undo  Put a recorded batch of renames back
  help  Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  <PATH>...
          Files and/or directories to clean

Options:
  -x, --exec
          Perform the renames, recording an undo journal

  -n, --dry-run
          Preview only; explicit form of the default

  -r, --recursive
          Descend into directories; without it, a directory argument has only its own name cleaned

      --on-collision <POLICY>
          What to do when two names want the same destination

          Possible values:
          - number: Insert `-N` before the extension, smallest free `N >= 2`
          - skip:   Leave every colliding entry alone and report it
          - fail:   Refuse the entire batch
          
          [default: number]

  -v, --verbose...
          List unchanged entries too

  -q, --quiet
          Errors only

      --json
          JSON on stdout, diagnostics on stderr

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Nothing is renamed unless you pass -x. Every -x run writes an undo journal to $XDG_STATE_HOME/detoxrs/journal; `detoxrs undo --last` reverts the most recent one.

Without -r, a directory argument has only its own name cleaned and nothing inside it is touched (detox differs).

Exit codes:
  0  everything requested was done; a preview with nothing wrong still exits 0
  1  something requested could not be done: an item failed or the batch aborted, a conflict was left unresolved, a subtree could not be read, or a rename broke a relative symlink
  2  usage, walk, or plan error (nothing was attempted at all)
